  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p><b>Deploy an AWS Directory Service AD Connector for integration.</b> is the best solution when you have an existing Microsoft Active Directory environment and need a straightforward way to connect AWS resources to it. AD Connector acts as a proxy, allowing AWS services to authenticate users against your corporate Active Directory without storing credentials in the cloud. This approach avoids complex synchronization or migration steps, which is ideal for organizations already heavily using Active Directory.</p><p><i>Set up a Simple AD using AWS Directory Service.</i> is incorrect because Simple AD is a standalone directory and does not allow native integration with an external Active Directory, making it unsuitable for organizations needing to leverage their current identity infrastructure.</p><p><i>Establish an AWS Directory Sync Domain operating through Lambda functions.</i> is not a valid AWS solution or best practice. There is no built-in 'Sync Domain' in combination with Lambda for this use-case, making this choice invalid.</p><p><i>Implement a Domain Sync feature using AWS Directory Service.</i> is also incorrect because AWS Directory Service does not offer a direct feature called \"Domain Sync.\" The most direct, managed solution for using your on-premises AD accounts on AWS is AD Connector, not custom or unofficial solutions.</p><ul><li><b>Tip:</b> Choose AD Connector whenever you want AWS to use your pre-existing corporate Active Directory accounts for authentication, with minimal operational overhead.</li><li>For organizations wanting AWS to host the entire directory, AWS Managed Microsoft AD is an option, but here the question focuses on keeping management simple and leveraging the current identity system directly.</li></ul>",
  "query": "<p>Your organization consists of over 2,000 technical staff. You're preparing to launch AWS adoption at scale and want to connect AWS with your company's established Microsoft Active Directory-based identity management system, which is extensively leveraged. What is the easiest approach to centrally manage AWS identities using your existing Active Directory setup?</p>",
